  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Definition
    
    The Frozen Peace denotes the current era on the Berg, beginning with the recession of organized orca raiding following the Orca Incursion. The name is misleading in the manner typical of penguin nomenclature: it describes not the presence of peace but the absence of existential siege. The era is better understood as a period of reconstruction under duress, marked by inter-cultural cooperation, institutional fragmentation, and a quiet determination that resists easy romanticization.
    
    ## Reconstruction and Cooperation
    
    With neither Aurora Stack nor Nightfall Shelf capable of rebuilding alone, the era's defining feature has been forced interdependence. Guilds that once guarded craft-secrets began sharing crystal-forging techniques and fire-core extraction methods — not from idealism but from the arithmetic of survival: too many specialists had died. Aurora sky-light channelers worked alongside Nightshelf fire-core miners in caverns still scarred from the last siege. The Flipper Accord, already renegotiated in substance if not in public acknowledgment, provided the framework.
    
    New traditions emerged from this proximity, blending Aurora mysticism with Nightshelf shadow-craft in forms neither culture had produced independently. Fissure☽ Aurora, a practice drawing on both aurora-light and deep-pressure traditions, was developed and formalized during this period. Formal agreements including the Foedus∴silenti and Foedus∴sper codified aspects of the new cooperative order.
    
    ## Festivals and Remembrance
    
    The great festivals returned — Accord (light-blessed) foremost among them — but transformed. Celebration now carries remembrance. At Umbra∴go memorials, penguins leave tokens for the fallen. The songs have different words.
    
    ## Character of the Era
    
    The Frozen Peace is not peaceful. The Wake-Singers remain active at the Corpse Current. Fire-core supply has grown unreliable as deep mines collapse. Corruption spreads through breach-points that cleansing rituals cannot permanently seal. Territorial control in the lower tunnels changes hands by force. Surface governance fragments.
    
    Every faction believes it is living through unprecedented times. The deep ice suggests otherwise.
    
    What distinguishes the era is its insistence on continuation. Colonies share rations in tunnel-camps. Workers descend into deep crevasses and return quieter, carrying the weight of every previous catastrophe the Berg has endured. Every new chick represents a victory against oblivion — though the ice, as always, reserves judgment on how long such victories last.
